import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Nav
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageCards
import ../../Ui/PageHero

## The ecosystem's overview: what a Roc Solid package is, how an app uses one, what a plugin is,
## and the packages there are, as cards in the sidebar's groups.
meta = {
	title: "The ecosystem",
	description: "Packages for Roc Solid apps: Roc modules and components you add to an app with one line in its header, and plugins that add routes, tables, jobs and scripts to the server. Twenty-four so far, among them sign-in, Stripe payments, Claude from the server, charts, forms, intl, Markdown, jobs and cron, email, an admin, guards, storage, Web Push and OpenTelemetry.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "packages", label: "The packages" },
		{ id: "halves", label: "What is in a package" },
		{ id: "use", label: "Adding one to an app" },
		{ id: "plugins", label: "Plugins" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem"
				title="Packages: Roc modules and components for any Roc Solid app"
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.ecosystem_charts} primary>Charts</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A package is a directory of Roc modules and, often, components, which you add to an app with one
				line in its header. Reach for one when your app needs something the platforms leave out: charts,
				form checks, translations, a job queue, email, an admin for your tables. Some packages are also
				plugins, which add routes, tables, background work or scripts to the server. There are twenty-four
				so far, each with a page here.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="packages">The packages</Heading>
			<p>
				They are grouped by where they do their work: in the page, on the server, and beside the app
				(tools, and watching the app run).
			</p>
			{...Nav.ecosystem_docs.groups.drop_if(|group| group.name == "").map(|group|
				<>
					<h3>{group.name}</h3>
					<PageCards pages={group.pages} />
				</>
			)}

			<Heading id="halves">What is in a package</Heading>
			<p>
				A package lives in its own directory (the ones above are in the repository's <code>packages/</code>,
				beside <code>dbcheck</code>, which <code>rocx check --sql</code> uses and no app imports). It can
				have two kinds of code, and many have both.
			</p>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Roc modules">
					<p>
						<code>main.roc</code> is an ordinary Roc package header that lists the package's modules. A
						package that only computes (charts, forms, intl, Markdown, QR codes) names no platform: its code
						can't render or fetch anything, so the server, a component in the browser and a test all call the
						same functions, and every function is an <code>expect</code> away from a test. A package that
						needs the server (to send email, run jobs or read the database) names the server platform
						instead, as more than half of them do.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Components">
					<p>
						<code>components/*.rocx</code> are components, such as <code>{"<BarChart>"}</code>. A component
						imports the platform's <code>Html</code> and <code>Solid</code>, and an app is two programs on
						two platforms: the server's and the browser's. So instead of being compiled in the package,
						the components are copied into the app by <code>rocx build</code>, and each half of the app
						compiles them on its own platform.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<p>
				A package that names the server platform can still have modules the browser uses (the audio
				package's <code>Audio.play!</code> is called from a click handler). It lists them in a second
				header, <code>browser.roc</code>, which names the browser platform when they need it (as audio's
				does) and no platform when they don't. The browser's half of the app sees that header instead of
				<code>main.roc</code>, so it can import only those modules. If a page imports one of the others,
				<code>rocx build</code> stops and says which import pulled it in.
			</p>

			<Heading id="use">Adding one to an app</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header, in <code>main.roc</code>, beside the platform. The name
				before the colon is the one you import it by; use the package's directory name, as every app in the
				repository does.
			</p>
			<Code title="main.roc" source={"app [program] { charts: \"../packages/charts/main.roc\", pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				That one line is enough for both halves. When <code>rocx build</code> writes the browser's copy of
				the app, it writes that copy's header from this one: the same packages, on the browser platform,
				each through its <code>browser.roc</code> if it has one. A package that names the server platform
				and has no <code>browser.roc</code> (Stripe, storage, PostHog) is left out of that copy, since the
				browser can import nothing of it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Then import what you need. A module is imported as in any Roc program
				(<code>import charts.Format</code>). A component is imported the same way
				(<code>import charts.BarChart</code>): <code>rocx build</code> has copied it to the app's
				<code>Generated/charts/BarChart.roc</code>, and points the import at that copy. A component's
				<code>{"<style>"}</code> goes into the app's stylesheet with the app's own, and the compiler's
				errors point at the package's file, not at the copy. Here is the charts page's bar chart, from
				<code>site/Demos/BarDemo.rocx</code>:
			</p>
			<Code source={Sources.charts_use.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Each package's page shows its own header line, its first example and its limits. Its
				<code>README.md</code>, in the package's directory, is the full reference.
			</p>

			<Heading id="plugins">Plugins</Heading>
			<p>
				Some features need more than functions to call. A job queue needs a table and workers that run in
				the background; a dev mailbox needs pages of its own; a sound player needs a script on every page.
				A package gives you these as a <strong>plugin</strong>: one value, made from options, that you list
				in your program's <code>plugins</code>.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc, in part"
				source={"import jobs.Job\nimport email.Email\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!,\n\tplugins: [Job.plugin({ jobs: App.jobs }), Email.plugin({})],\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				A plugin can add database tables (applied before the app's own migrations), background loops, work
				to do when the server starts, development pages (only under <code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code>), and files it serves, such as a script every page
				loads or a service worker. It adds no routes: every URL your app answers is a file under your
				<code>routes/</code> (D-116), so a package that answers requests gives you handlers to call from route
				files of your own, where you want them and behind the middleware of the folder you put them in. A
				plugin can also say what it needs: an environment variable, a database, or another plugin. When the
				server starts it checks every plugin, and any problem (a missing variable, a plugin that needs one
				you didn't list, two files at one place) stops it with the whole list. The
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>server platform's page</a> covers what a plugin can
				add and how to write one.
			</p>
			<p>The packages that are plugins, and what each one adds:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_auth}>Auth</a>: <code>{"Auth.plugin({ providers })"}</code> adds the sessions' tables; its handlers answer your routes for a mailed link, a provider and back, the second factor, signing out and the dev sign-in, with your <code>admit!</code>. Its middleware hands the signed-in user on.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_stripe}>Stripe</a>: <code>{"Stripe.plugin({})"}</code> adds the tables of customers, checkouts and subscriptions it keeps in step; <code>{"Stripe.webhook_route!"}</code> answers your route for the webhook Stripe sends its events to, with your <code>on_event!</code>. Its functions send people to Checkout and the Customer Portal, and say who has paid.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_ai}>AI</a>: <code>{"Ai.plugin({})"}</code> keeps the API's key and the model when the server starts (a server outside <code>--dev</code> doesn't start without a key), and once started logs which model it asks. Its functions ask Claude: an answer whole or streamed to a page, an answer read into a Roc type, and the app's own functions as tools. Its agents are models with a role and tools of their own, and an agent can be another agent's tool.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>Jobs and cron</a>: <code>{"Job.plugin({ jobs: App.jobs, crons })"}</code> adds the queue's table, its workers, the schedules' loop, and a sweep that removes finished jobs after a while.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>Email</a>: <code>{"Email.plugin({})"}</code> adds the dev mailbox under <code>--dev</code>, and once the server has started, logs whether the SMTP server answers. Outside <code>--dev</code>, a server with no <code>SMTP_URL</code> doesn't start.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>Push</a>: <code>{"Push.plugin({})"}</code> adds the subscriptions' table, the job that sends notifications (it needs the jobs plugin), the service worker and the page's script. Under <code>--dev</code> with no VAPID keys, it makes a pair for the run. Its three handlers answer your routes for the key and a page's subscriptions.</li>
								<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_guard}>Guard</a>: <code>{"FlagStore.plugin({})"}</code> keeps feature-flag overrides in a table, so they survive a restart. The rate limits and guards need no plugin.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>Audio</a>: <code>{"AudioPlayer.plugin({})"}</code> adds the player for <code>Audio.play!</code>, a script every page loads.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_vibrate}>Vibrate</a>: <code>{"Vibrator.plugin({})"}</code> adds the script that passes <code>Vibrate.pattern!</code> to the browser's vibration motor. An app on a phone needs none.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_posthog}>PostHog</a>: <code>{"PostHog.plugin({})"}</code> sends errors to PostHog's error tracking; its handlers answer your routes for the page's script and the endpoint it reports to.</li>
				<li><a href={Paths.ecosystem_otel}>OpenTelemetry</a>: <code>{"Otel.plugin({})"}</code> reads the <code>OTEL_*</code> settings when the server starts, and adds the loop that adds up the app's measurements and sends them to the collector. Its middleware times every request.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
